@*
    One act on an outside tool: its readiness, and its lifecycle.

    There is no `bool Disabled` here, and its absence is the design. Every other
    button in this library takes one, which is why an unavailable integration
    looks different on every surface that has one: a greyed control says nothing
    about why, so each host invents its own tooltip, or its own hidden branch, or
    nothing at all. The only way to make this inert is to hand it an
    IntegrationReadiness, and that record cannot be constructed without a cause —
    so the bare disabled integration button is not a thing a host can produce.

    Always AppButton, never IconButton. AppButton.Busy already embeds Spinner,
    sets aria-busy and disables the control without taking it out of the tab
    order, so focus survives the round trip; a second loading button here would
    be a second thing to get wrong in a family whose whole point is not having
    two of anything. Compact density is `btn btn--icon` plus Size=Small, which is
    IconButton's own class stem, so the shape is unchanged. What is copied from
    IconButton is its convention rather than its markup: Title falls back to the
    accessible name, and an icon-only button is never label-less.

    The host owns the state. This component advances nothing on its own, for the
    same reason MarkdownView never mutates a block: the truth is upstream, and a
    component that guessed at it would eventually disagree with the thing that
    knows. Which is also why the succeeded and failed lines do not auto-clear the
    way CopyButton's does. A copy is finished the moment the clipboard returns
    and there is nothing further to do; creating an issue produces an issue, and
    a line that says "done" and then vanishes takes the link to it with it.

    The button is never removed — not while unavailable, not while running, not
    after it failed. A reader who cannot find the control concludes the product
    cannot do the thing at all, and a control that moves out from under a pointer
    mid-act is worse than a row one item too long.
*@

<span class="@ClassValue" data-testid="@TestId">
    @if (!Ready.IsAvailable)
    {
        @* Disabled, and described. The reason reaches the pointer through
           `title` and assistive technology through aria-describedby, because
           neither route alone reaches everybody: a title is not announced
           reliably, and a described-by is invisible. *@
        <AppButton Variant="@Variant"
                   Size="@Size"
                   BaseClass="@BaseClass"
                   Disabled="true"
                   TestId="@ButtonTestId"
                   title="@Reason"
                   aria-label="@Action.Label"
                   aria-describedby="@_reasonId">
            <Icon>@ActionMark()</Icon>
            <ChildContent>@Caption(Action.Label)</ChildContent>
        </AppButton>

        <span id="@_reasonId" class="integration-action__reason" data-testid="@ReasonTestId">@Reason</span>

        @if (OnRemedy.HasDelegate && Remedy is { Length: > 0 } remedy)
        {
            <AppButton Variant="ButtonVariant.Ghost"
                       Size="ButtonSize.Small"
                       CssClass="integration-action__remedy"
                       TestId="@RemedyTestId"
                       OnClick="() => OnRemedy.InvokeAsync(Ready)">@remedy</AppButton>
        }
    }
    else
    {
        <AppButton Variant="@Variant"
                   Size="@Size"
                   BaseClass="@BaseClass"
                   Busy="@(Action.State is IntegrationActionState.Running)"
                   BusyLabel="@($"{Action.Label}…")"
                   TestId="@(Action.State is IntegrationActionState.Confirming ? ConfirmTestId ?? ButtonTestId : ButtonTestId)"
                   title="@TitleValue"
                   aria-label="@AccessibleName"
                   OnClick="() => OnInvoke.InvokeAsync(Action)">
            <Icon>@ActionMark()</Icon>
            <ChildContent>@Caption(VisibleLabel)</ChildContent>
        </AppButton>

        @if (Action.State is IntegrationActionState.Confirming)
        {
            @* In place, beside the act, rather than in a dialog. A confirm
               dialog that previews what would actually be sent is the right home
               for a preview and is not built yet; ConfirmDialog already routes
               Escape and backdrop dismissal through Cancel, so it would inherit
               that for free when it is. The inline confirm is the floor. *@
            <AppButton Variant="ButtonVariant.Ghost"
                       Size="@Size"
                       CssClass="integration-action__cancel"
                       TestId="@CancelTestId"
                       OnClick="() => OnCancel.InvokeAsync(Action)">Cancel</AppButton>
        }

        @if (Action.State is IntegrationActionState.Succeeded)
        {
            <span class="integration-action__status integration-action__status--succeeded"
                  role="status"
                  data-testid="@StatusTestId">
                <span class="integration-action__status-glyph" aria-hidden="true">@SuccessGlyph()</span>
                @if (SuccessContent is not null)
                {
                    @SuccessContent
                }
                else
                {
                    @StatusText
                }
            </span>
        }

        @if (Action.State is IntegrationActionState.Failed)
        {
            @* role="alert" here and nowhere else in this family. The four
               unavailable causes are standing conditions and stay statuses; this
               one is an event, and it happened because the reader pressed
               something. Toast already draws that line between Error/Warning and
               everything else, so this follows it rather than inventing a second
               rule. *@
            <span class="integration-action__status integration-action__status--failed"
                  role="alert"
                  data-testid="@StatusTestId">
                <span class="integration-action__status-glyph" aria-hidden="true">@FailureGlyph()</span>
                @StatusText
            </span>

            @* Retry raises its own callback rather than re-raising invoke, so a
               host can tell a first attempt from a second in whatever it logs. *@
            <AppButton Variant="ButtonVariant.Ghost"
                       Size="@Size"
                       CssClass="integration-action__retry"
                       TestId="@RetryTestId"
                       OnClick="() => OnRetry.InvokeAsync(Action)">Retry</AppButton>
        }
    }
</span>

@code {
    /// <summary>Unique per instance rather than derived from the action's id: two
    /// bars on one page may legitimately carry the same act, and two elements
    /// with one id would send aria-describedby to whichever the browser saw
    /// first.</summary>
    private readonly string _reasonId = $"integration-reason-{Guid.NewGuid():N}";

    [Parameter, EditorRequired]
    public IntegrationActionSpec Action { get; set; } = default!;

    [Parameter]
    public IntegrationDensity Density { get; set; } = IntegrationDensity.Toolbar;

    /// <summary>Replaces the succeeded line's text, so a host can put the
    /// consequence in it — "Issue #128 created" with the number as a link. The
    /// act produced something; the line is where it lives until the host clears
    /// the state.</summary>
    [Parameter]
    public RenderFragment? SuccessContent { get; set; }

    [Parameter]
    public EventCallback<IntegrationActionSpec> OnInvoke { get; set; }

    [Parameter]
    public EventCallback<IntegrationActionSpec> OnCancel { get; set; }

    [Parameter]
    public EventCallback<IntegrationActionSpec> OnRetry { get; set; }

    /// <summary>Raised with the readiness rather than with the action, because
    /// the remedy is about the cause: four acts blocked by one expired token have
    /// one thing to fix between them.</summary>
    [Parameter]
    public EventCallback<IntegrationReadiness> OnRemedy { get; set; }

    [Parameter]
    public string? CssClass { get; set; }

    [Parameter]
    public string? TestId { get; set; }

    [Parameter]
    public string? ButtonTestId { get; set; }

    [Parameter]
    public string? ConfirmTestId { get; set; }

    [Parameter]
    public string? CancelTestId { get; set; }

    [Parameter]
    public string? RetryTestId { get; set; }

    [Parameter]
    public string? StatusTestId { get; set; }

    [Parameter]
    public string? ReasonTestId { get; set; }

    [Parameter]
    public string? RemedyTestId { get; set; }

    private IntegrationReadiness Ready => Action.ReadinessOrReady;

    private string Reason => IntegrationStates.ReasonFor(Ready);

    private string? Remedy => IntegrationStates.RemedyFor(Ready);

    private bool IconOnly => IntegrationStates.IsIconOnly(Density);

    private ButtonSize Size => IntegrationStates.SizeFor(Density);

    private string BaseClass => IntegrationStates.ButtonBaseClassFor(Density);

    /// <summary>Primary for an act the surface leads with, danger only once a
    /// destructive act is asking to be confirmed. An idle destructive act still
    /// looks like an act: painting it red before anyone pressed it would make
    /// every surface that offers one look alarming at rest.</summary>
    private ButtonVariant Variant => Action.State switch
    {
        IntegrationActionState.Confirming when Action.Destructive => ButtonVariant.Danger,
        IntegrationActionState.Confirming => ButtonVariant.Primary,
        _ => Action.Prominence is IntegrationProminence.Primary ? ButtonVariant.Primary : ButtonVariant.Secondary
    };

    private string VisibleLabel => Action.State is IntegrationActionState.Confirming
        ? Action.ConfirmLabel ?? "Confirm"
        : Action.Label;

    /// <summary>The name assistive technology reads. It never collapses to the
    /// confirm word alone — "Confirm" on its own tells a reader who arrived by
    /// keyboard nothing about what they would be confirming.</summary>
    private string AccessibleName => Action.State is IntegrationActionState.Confirming
        ? $"{VisibleLabel} — {Action.Label}"
        : Action.Label;

    /// <summary>IconButton's rule: a title only where it says something the
    /// visible label does not. At Compact density the label is not visible, so
    /// the title is what a pointer user gets instead.</summary>
    private string? TitleValue
    {
        get
        {
            if (!string.IsNullOrWhiteSpace(Action.Description)) return Action.Description;

            return IconOnly ? AccessibleName : null;
        }
    }

    private string StatusText => string.IsNullOrWhiteSpace(Action.StatusMessage)
        ? Action.State is IntegrationActionState.Failed
            ? $"{Action.Label} — failed"
            : $"{Action.Label} — done"
        : Action.StatusMessage;

    private string ClassValue
    {
        get
        {
            var stem = $"integration-action integration-action--{IntegrationStates.SlugOf(Density)}";
            return string.IsNullOrWhiteSpace(CssClass) ? stem : $"{stem} {CssClass}";
        }
    }

    /// <summary>Nothing at all at a density that shows the label, because the
    /// caption is the label; the visually-hidden route is not needed when the
    /// word is on screen.</summary>
    private RenderFragment Caption(string label) =>
        @<text>@if (!IconOnly) { <span class="integration-action__label">@label</span> }</text>;

    /// <summary>The provider's mark, or a neutral outward-act glyph where there
    /// is no provider. A compact button with no provider would otherwise render
    /// empty — an icon-only control with no icon — which is the one shape this
    /// component must never produce.</summary>
    private RenderFragment ActionMark() =>
        @<text>
            @if (Action.Provider is not IntegrationProvider.None)
            {
                <ProviderMark Provider="@Action.Provider" Title="@(IconOnly ? IntegrationStates.NameOf(Action.Provider) : null)" />
            }
            else
            {
                <svg viewBox="0 0 16 16" width="16" height="16" fill="none" stroke="currentColor"
                     stroke-width="1.6" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"
                     aria-hidden="true" focusable="false">
                    <path d="M9.25 2.75h4v4" />
                    <path d="M13.25 2.75 7.5 8.5" />
                    <path d="M12 9.75v2.5a1.5 1.5 0 0 1-1.5 1.5h-6.5a1.5 1.5 0 0 1-1.5-1.5v-6.5a1.5 1.5 0 0 1 1.5-1.5h2.5" />
                </svg>
            }
        </text>;

    private RenderFragment SuccessGlyph() =>
        @<svg viewBox="0 0 16 16" width="14" height="14" fill="none" stroke="currentColor"
              stroke-width="1.6" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"
              aria-hidden="true" focusable="false">
            <path d="M3.25 8.5 6.25 11.5l6.5-7" />
        </svg>;

    private RenderFragment FailureGlyph() =>
        @<svg viewBox="0 0 16 16" width="14" height="14" fill="none" stroke="currentColor"
              stroke-width="1.6" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"
              aria-hidden="true" focusable="false">
            <circle cx="8" cy="8" r="5.25" />
            <path d="M6.25 6.25 9.75 9.75" />
            <path d="M9.75 6.25 6.25 9.75" />
        </svg>;
}
